From 86c2934713b1db45ee50b87033a3ec329f36eb9e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrew Youll <andrewyoull86@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 23:25:48 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 17/17] swscale arm: scaling to RGB32 in NEON (fast bilinear,
 yuv2rgbx32)

With SWS_FAST_BILINEAR to RGB32/BGR32 (RGBA/BGRA on little-endian) the
32-bit ARM path was C: the fast bilinear horizontal scalers and the
packed RGB output. Now, bit-exact with the C:
 - ff_hyscale_fast/ff_hcscale_fast: 8 outputs a step, the positions and
   weights in NEON and the source bytes through vtbl, for scales down to
   about 1.85x (the rest of the line, and larger scales, in C);
 - yuv2rgbx32 _1 and _2: the 32 bpp tables as arithmetic,
   clip((Y * cy + k + ((clip(C) * c) >> 16) * cy) >> 16) per channel,
   which ff_yuv2rgb_c_init_tables now also stores
   (SwsContext.yuv2rgb_arith, _ok where it applies and fits 32 bits).
Byte-element loads and stores only.
---
 libswscale/arm/Makefile          |   1 +
 libswscale/arm/scaled_rgb_neon.S | 257 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 libswscale/arm/swscale.c         | 129 ++++++++++++++++
 libswscale/swscale_internal.h    |   9 ++
 libswscale/yuv2rgb.c             |  21 +++
 5 files changed, 417 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 libswscale/arm/scaled_rgb_neon.S

diff --git a/libswscale/arm/Makefile b/libswscale/arm/Makefile
index 792da6b..d31987f 100644
--- a/libswscale/arm/Makefile
+++ b/libswscale/arm/Makefile
@@ -3,6 +3,7 @@ OBJS        += arm/swscale.o                    \
 
 NEON-OBJS   += arm/rgb2yuv_neon_32.o
 NEON-OBJS   += arm/rgb2yuv_neon_16.o
+NEON-OBJS   += arm/scaled_rgb_neon.o
 NEON-OBJS   += arm/hscale.o                     \
                arm/output.o                     \
                arm/yuv2rgb_neon.o               \
diff --git a/libswscale/arm/scaled_rgb_neon.S b/libswscale/arm/scaled_rgb_neon.S
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..35c3112
--- /dev/null
+++ b/libswscale/arm/scaled_rgb_neon.S
@@ -0,0 +1,257 @@
+/*
+ * swscale, scaling to RGB32 in NEON for 32-bit ARM:
+ *  - the fast bilinear horizontal scalers (ff_hyscale_fast_c,
+ *    ff_hcscale_fast_c), for up to about 1.85x down;
+ *  - yuv2rgbx32 packed output (_1 and _2 vertical steps), the tables
+ *    worked out as arithmetic (SwsContext.yuv2rgb_arith).
+ * Both bit-exact with the C. Byte-element loads and stores only (RISC OS
+ * traps unaligned wider-element accesses).
+ *
